# RDS (Relational Database Service) 4:25
Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) is a fully managed database service provided by AWS. 
It allows you to create, operate, and scale relational databases without managing the underlying infrastructure.

> Real-Life Example
Imagine you have an E-commerce Website:
Users sign up.
Products are stored.
Orders are placed.
Payments are recorded.

All this structured data is stored in a database.

Instead of installing MySQL on your own server and managing backups, updates, and security, you use Amazon RDS, and AWS handles all these tasks.

> Benefits of Using RDS:
High availability and fault tolerance.
Vertical and Horizontal Scaling.
Automated backups and recovery.
Read replicas for improved read performance.
Multi AZ setup for DR (Disaster Recovery).
Cost-effectiveness.

> Use Cases
1. 🛒 E-Commerce Website
Stores:
Users
Products
Orders
Payments
2. 🏦 Banking Application
Stores:
Customer Accounts
Transactions
Loan Details
Account Balance

> Why Use RDS?
Without RDS:
Install database manually
Take backups manually
Update database manually
Handle crashes manually

With RDS:
AWS installs the database
AWS takes backups
AWS updates the software

> Supported Database Engines
MySQL
PostgreSQL
MariaDB
Oracle
Microsoft SQL Server

> Advantages
Easy to set up
Automatic backups
High availability
Automatic updates
Easy scaling

> Limitations
No OS-level access (no SSH into the database server)
Less control compared to self-managed databases on EC2

> RDS vs EC2 Database
Amazon RDS	Database on EC2
Fully managed	Self-managed
Automatic backups	Manual backups
Automatic patching	Manual patching
Easy scaling	Manual scaling


Q2. What databases does RDS support?
Answer: MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, SQL Server, and Amazon Aurora.

Q3. What is Multi-AZ in RDS?
Answer: Multi-AZ creates a standby database in another Availability Zone for high availability and automatic failover.

Q4. What is a Read Replica?
Answer: A Read Replica is a read-only copy of an RDS database used to improve read performance and distribute read traffic.

Q5. Can we SSH into an RDS instance?
Answer: No. RDS is a managed service, so AWS does not provide SSH access to the underlying server.

# RDS Instance
Create an RDS MySQL Instance:
Use Free Tier
Username will be admin and you can set a password (don't use special characters)
Keep Public Access = True to access it from a local machine or remote server
Create a Security Group and allow Port 3306 from everywhere (0.0.0.0/0) (for learning purposes)
After creating the instance, copy the Endpoint (Hostname) to connect to the database

# EC2 Instance
sudo yum install -y docker
sudo service docker start
sudo usermod -aG docker ec2-user
sudo docker pull philippaul/node-mysql-app:02

Note: Allowing 3306 from everywhere is suitable only for practice or demo environments. In production, restrict access to specific IP addresses or security groups for better security.


# AWS DynamoDM
AWS DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service provided by AWS that stores data in key-value and document formats. 
It offers high performance, automatic scaling, low latency, and high availability, making it ideal for modern applications that require fast and reliable data access.

> AWS DynamoDB Free Tier
DynamoDB offers a Free Tier that includes 25 GB of storage.
The Free Tier also provides 25 Write Capacity Units (WCU) and 25 Read Capacity Units (RCU).
This is sufficient to handle up to 200 million requests per month for many small applications.

> Benefits of AWS DynamoDB
Serverless: No need for server provisioning, software installation, maintenance, or patching.
Automatic Scaling: Instantly scales up or down based on demand, with no manual adjustments needed.
Zero Downtime: Provides continuous availability without maintenance windows.
On-Demand Pricing: Pay only for the read/write requests used, ideal for fluctuating workloads.
Idle Cost Savings: Scales down to zero during inactivity, so there’s no cost when tables have no traffic.

